      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$, then ${A}^{4}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${A}^{2}=A,$ so $A$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & ={A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
